  "body": {
    "id": "thm:profiled-ar-length",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For a finite \\(C_\\alpha>0\\) depending only on \\(\\alpha\\) and the fixed class constants, \\[\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal M_N(u)}\\frac{E_P[\\ell(C_N^{\\mathrm{AR}})]}{\\min\\{1,u^{-1/2}\\}}\\le C_\\alpha\\] uniformly over admissible \\(u=u_N>0\\) with nonempty \\(\\mathcal M_N(u)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "alpha",
      "N",
      "P",
      "MNu",
      "u",
      "ell",
      "CAR"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:strength-law-class",
      "def:profiled-ar-set",
      "thm:profiled-ar-honesty",
      "lem:collection-information-modulus"
    ],
    "justification": "The confidence interval automatically shortens at the collection-information rate.",
    "gap": "Weak-IV inversion results do not establish bounded-ratio expected-length rates under a trial-support hypergraph.",
    "consumer": "Evidence-synthesis teams can value a trial that covers only selected target cells.",
    "proof_tex": "Put \\(m_P(\\theta)=\\mu_D(\\theta_T-\\theta)\\), as follows from the transported residual-contrast identity. Integrating the bounded exponential-moment estimates in the honesty proof gives random variables \\(\\Delta_N\\) satisfying\n\\[\n \\sup_{\\theta\\in[-1,1]}|\\widehat m_N(\\theta)-m_P(\\theta)|\n \\le\\Delta_N,\\qquad E_P\\Delta_N\\le CN^{-1/2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n E_P\\sup_{\\theta\\in[-1,1]}\\sqrt{\\widehat V_N(\\theta)}\n \\le CN^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nThe exceptional small-count event contributes at most \\(Ce^{-cN}\\), since the fallback price and denominator truncation make the empirical quantities bounded.\n\nLet \\(z_\\alpha=\\sqrt{\\chi^2_{1,1-\\alpha}}\\). If \\(\\theta\\) is accepted, then\n\\[\n \\mu_D|\\theta-\\theta_T|\n =|m_P(\\theta)|\n \\le\\Delta_N+z_\\alpha\\sqrt{\\widehat V_N(\\theta)}.\n\\]\nThus every accepted point, and therefore both endpoints of its hull, lies in the corresponding random neighborhood of \\(\\theta_T\\). Since the whole interval lies in \\([-1,1]\\),\n\\[\n E_P\\ell(C_N^{\\mathrm{AR}})\n \\le C_\\alpha\\min\\left\\{1,\\frac1{\\mu_D\\sqrt N}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe deterministic lower variance bound \\(V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P,\\theta_T)\\ge v_0/N\\) implies\n\\[\n \\frac1{\\mu_D\\sqrt N}\n \\le\\frac1{\\sqrt{v_0}}\n \\frac{\\sqrt{V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P,\\theta_T)}}{\\mu_D}\n =\\frac1{\\sqrt{v_0T_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P)}}.\n\\]\nFor \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_N(u)\\), this is at most \\(v_0^{-1/2}u^{-1/2}\\). Taking the supremum over the class and then the upper limit proves the stated uniform bound.",
